            if (Old_arrListContainsX) {
	            Assert.GreaterOrEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, -1);
            } else {
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, -1);
            }

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  new array list index of x is more than or same as -1 
            if (New_arrListContainsX) {
	            Assert.True(New_arrListIndexOfX >= -1);
            } else {
                Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListIndexOfX, -1);
            }

            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_x, arrList[Old_index]);
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_index, New_arrListIndexOfX);
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_index, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
```

This test method tests the contract of ArrayList's Set method, where Set modifies the value of a specific element. For this to work, it is assumed that `index` specified corresponds to a valid element in the ArrayList `arrList`. 

The precondition assumes `arrList.Count` is the count before the modification, `index` is the position to set value x, and `x` is the value you're setting it to. The `PexObserve` utility methods are used to provide useful values on the output screen for debugging purposes.

Finally, several assertions ensure the correctness of the method implementation. 
- We assert `Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount` to ensure the count of the ArrayList stays the same. 
- We assert `Old_x == arrList[Old_index]` to make sure the new value was correctly set.
- We assert `Old_index == New_arrListIndexOfX` and `Old_index == New_arrListLastIndexOfX` to verify the index returned by `IndexOf` and `LastIndexOf` remain the same and are equal.
- We assert `Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX` to ensure `Contains` result does not change.
- We assert `Old_arrListIndexOfX >= -1` and `New_arrListIndexOfX >= -1` to ensure that the index is not less than -1.

The test is marked as `[PexMethod(Action = Action.NoOp)]` to ensure that Pex will not provide any additional inputs for this method.

If any of these assertions fail, they will cause an exception and this will be indicated in the output window.

This helps in identifying any bugs in your code.

## Testing the [GET] Item Property

This example tests the [GET] Item Property method of your ArrayList class. 

Here is an example method for testing the item property:

```c#
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestClass]
    public class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [TestMethod]
        [PexMethod(Action = Action.NoOp)]
        public void GET_ItemContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

            int value = arrList[index];
            Assert.AreEqual(value, arrList[index]);
        }
    }
}
```

This test method tests the GET Item Property contract. For this to work, it is assumed that `index` specified correspond to a valid element in the ArrayList `arrList`.

The precondition ensures that index is less than the size of the ArrayList and greater than or equal to 0. 

Finally, the assertion ensures that the value retrieved from the index matches the value in ArrayList at that index. If different, the assertion will fail and an exception will be thrown.

Similar to the above example, mark the test method with `[PexMethod(Action = Action.NoOp)]` to ensure that Pex does not provide any additional inputs for this method.

If the assertion fails, a detailed exception will appear in the output window. This will help you to identify any bugs in your code.

Remember to replace the example `ArrayList` class you used in your test with your own class or the one you're using.


