Kindly help me understand where my code is wrong. I also need to understand how to make these properties generic. I may be trying to access properties that do not exist. The Pex has no error in the test but it shows "Method GET_SetContract is expected to return a result. If PexMethodAttribute.IsStatic is true, then it could be that the method is not static. Please, provide more information". The same applies to the PUT_SetContract method.

A: The Pex framework is designed to create a comprehensive suite of tests including edge cases. It does not handle all possible scenarios, so it has its limitations depending on the complexity of the code you are examining.
The first thing you might check: is your code well-designed and correct?


*

*Does your class correctly implement the ArrayList contract, as defined in the [PexClass] attribute?

*Are all required fields properly implemented and correctly initialized?

*Do your methods behave as per the contract defined?


As for your second question, to make these properties generic, you simply change your methods to be generic instead of accepting ArrayList. Something like this:
[PexMethod(MaxRuns = 000000)]
public T GET_SetContract<T>([PexAssumeUnderTest]List<T> list, int index)
{
    PexAssume.True(index >= 0 && index < list.Count);

    T OldValue = list[index];
    return OldValue;
}

Note that you may need to adjust your assertions to make them valid for a generic list.
Let me know if you have any other questions on this. I'll be glad to provide further help. Good luck in your testing endeavors, and don't hesitate to ask if you have additional questions or if something seems unclear to you.

A: It seems like you are trying to use parameterized testing and are using the ArrayList class, which is not parameterized and may have issues. 
Consider replacing your ArrayList with the generic List<T> class instead.
Your code will look something like this:
public void PUT_SetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]List<int> list, int x, int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < list.Count);

    // rest of your code...
}

For the second method, you could use it like this:
public T GET_SetContract<T>([PexAssumeUnderTest]List<T> list, int index)
{
    PexAssume.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < list.Count);

    T OldValue = list[index];
    return OldValue;
}

And the usage of this might look like this (the type is inferred):
MyClass myObject = new MyClass();
myObject.GET_SetContract<int>(listOfIntegers, 2); //where listOfIntegers is a List<int>

Hope this can help you, let me know if you need more assistance.

