A: If you are using ArrayList and you call Add() to it, Count will increase and there will be space for that element to be added. But this doesn't mean that you can change the value at the index you have given, because this place could be occupied by another element. You would get a run-time exception if you tried to do that (the element at this index does not belong to the ArrayList).
In the case where you do want to store different values in elements at the given index, you might be best off using a Dictionary<TKey, TValue> or even a List<T>, if order matters.
However, if you really want to work with ArrayList in the way its Add(T value) and this[int index] properties seem to work, you could do something like:
var temp = arrList[index]; // store old value
arrList[index] = x; // override old value
arrList.Add(temp); // re-add old value to keep count going up

This way you would simulate the usual workings of ArrayList while still supporting your chosen contract.
Alternatively, you might consider using List<T> which has the same Add and indexer member, but you can change elements:
var list = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
list[2] = 6; // list becomes {1, 2, 6, 4, 5}

If you really want a random access list of int, List<int> would be the way to go.
Just remember, in C# there are different types (ArrayList, List<>, Dictionary<> etc.) designed with different purposes. You would use them very differently, and a solution could also affect the performance of your programs. 

A: The behaviour of ArrayList and Dictionary are different than arrays. They have the ability to handle dynamic size and add/remove elements to/from them. However, they don't provide the index access to elements like in an array. 
The reason you get run time error is because ArrayList doesn't maintain the order of elements.
In your example, you've used ArrayList and tried to access index after adding a new value. The ArrayList doesn't reserve this space for a new value because it doesn't need to maintain the order of elements. That's why you get an 'index out of range' error when attempting to access it. 

In order to do this type of test, you would have to use a List<int>, because you can manipulate its elements at arbitrary indices and it maintains the order of its elements:
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_SetContract(List<int> list, int x, int index)
{
    [...]
}

Also, remember to use [PexMethod(MaxExceptions = n)] in public methods, where n is the maximum number of exceptions you expect to occur in your method.

