When we run the above program in Pex, it will use the existing data in the ArrayList to generate inputs and run the test and it will also capture the results and compare with the expected results and will provide the result as pass or fail. This concept where the Pex takes the existing data from the input to generate the test case(s) is known as Pex driven contract testing.

This is one of the simplest ways to do Contract Testing and it'll also work for any other data type which is important as often the contracts we are testing with have a specific type of input or output.

Pex is also powerful with many other assert options that you can explore, the important part to get started is the understanding of the pre-condition and post-condition which is used to ensure the contracts of the method are met. 

It is important to note that while Pex Contract Testing helps in providing more accurate results, it's not perfect as sometimes you may still find edge-cases and specific boundary scenarios it can't cover. So, use other testing tools for these specific cases.

I hope this makes the understanding of Pex Contract Testing clearer to you. Do you have any more queries?

