A: If you want to test the index out-of-range case, one way would be to add the following method:
public void SetContract_OutOfRange([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, [PexRange(30,30)]int index)
{
    // this will force a specific out-of-range index
    Assume.That(index < 0 || index >= arrList.Count);

    Action action = () => arrList[index] = 1; // should throw

    Assert.That(action, Throws.InstanceOf<ArgumentException>(), "No exception was thrown");
    Assert.That(arrList.Count, Is.EqualTo(arrList.Count), "The count after error did not change");
}

This approach uses the Assume.That method and a conditional lambda expression to trigger an ArgumentException during the action part of the test. The lambda checks an action that accesses an array element out of bounds, and it should throw an exception. Assert.That is used to verify the expected exception (ArgumentException) was thrown and the count did not change as a side effect. 
It also uses NUnit's Assert library with the Throws and Is classes to validate the expected result.
This should give you an idea of how to test the index out-of-range case. 
Remember that PEX does not consider negative indices and indices equal to Count to be out-of-range, that's why we need to use Assume.That method to specifically test this situation.
Q: How to convert date in "YYYY-MM-DD" to "dd MM, YYYY" in SQL Server? I have a datetime value "2020-07-25 00:00:00.000" that I would like to convert to "25 Jul, 2020".
Is there any SQL Server function that can help me to directly convert the date? I could not find any function online.

A: SQL Server has a function STRTRANS that can convert between day-month-year format, day-month-year (or year-month-day), month-day-year, etc. For your example you'd use it like this:
DECLARE @date DATETIME = '2020-07-25 00:00:00.000';

SELECT 
    FORMAT (@date, 'dd MM, yyyy') AS 'Date',
    STRTRANS (CONVERT(VARCHAR(12), @date, 106), 'ENU', 'EUD') AS 'Translation'

This will return:
Date       Translation
25 Jul, 2020   --------------------            // and in new array list index of x is more than or same as -1

